};

const gdStudioSpot = {
  "prompt": "One line of this studio note is wrong — click it:",
  "lines": [
    "Unlike graphics, the interactive layer is just rules — and rules can be run by hand.",
    "A spreadsheet economy can be playtested over a lunch table.",
    "A combat system's pacing can be felt with tokens.",
    "Building first is the cheapest way to learn whether the loop works.",
    "And building first teaches its lesson too late to act on."
  ],
  "errorLine": 3,
  "correction": "Building first means paying full price — art, code, engine work — to learn what a deck of cards would have taught in an afternoon.",
  "why": "The finished game is the real thing, but the loop was testable for pocket change — which is why the field's hard-won rule is to test the loop as cheaply as possible."
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/game-development/foundational/game-design-foundations">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is the first lesson of Game Development, and it deliberately contains no code, no
    engine and no mathematics beyond a sketch. Design comes first because every later
    lesson — engines, graphics, AI, level design — is downstream of decisions made here.
    You need no prerequisites except games you have played and an interest in why they felt
    the way they felt.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>define what makes a game — and distinguish one from a toy, a puzzle and a film;</li>
    <li>use the MDA framework (Mechanics–Dynamics–Aesthetics) both to analyse games and to design them backwards from a feeling;</li>
    <li>explain the core loop, flow theory and the difference between positive and negative feedback — and what each sign does to a game session;</li>
    <li>write a one-breath core-loop statement and know why modern design documents stay short;</li>
    <li>say what a playtest can tell you that no amount of designer argument can.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    One habit throughout: separate what you <em>write</em> (rules) from what the
    <em>player does</em> (play) from what the player <em>feels</em> (experience). Most
    beginner design mistakes are one of these three wearing another's clothes.
  </p>

  <h2>The designer who is never in the room</h2>
  <p>
    Watch someone play a game you helped make and you learn the strange constraint of the
    craft: you are not there. The player never meets the designer — they meet the rules.
    Every emotion the game produces, the designer purchased in advance by writing those
    rules and hoping. Game design is the craft of engineering emotion through rules: every
    other design discipline works on matter or information, but this one works on the
    player's own attention, at a distance, through a machine that runs on a human mind.
  </p>
  <p>
    The distance is why the field needs theory at all. Intuition says "make it fun", but
    fun is a result, not a control — no editor panel exposes it. The discipline's shared
    framework, <strong>MDA</strong> (Mechanics–Dynamics–Aesthetics), names the causal chain
    that stands between the designer and the feeling: write <em>mechanics</em> (the rules),
    watch players turn them into <em>dynamics</em> (play behaviour), and hope those
    dynamics produce the intended <em>aesthetics</em> (the felt experience). Design is
    arguing backwards along that chain — from a feeling you want, to behaviour that would
    cause it, to rules that would cause the behaviour. Everything in this lesson is that
    argument, made rigorous.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the MDA framework, read both directions" widget="equationStep" config={mdaCfg} />

  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the widget lists the chain in the order the
    <em>player</em> experiences it — mechanics first, aesthetics last (steps 1–3) — and
    then in the order the <em>designer</em> must work it: feeling first, rules last (step
    4). Step 5 is the honesty clause. The argument from aesthetics to mechanics is a
    hypothesis, and the playtest is the experiment; the designer proposes, the players
    dispose.
  </p>

  <h2>What makes a game</h2>
  <p>
    The classic scholarly definition (Salen and Zimmerman's <em>Rules of Play</em>) runs: a
    game is a system in which players engage in artificial conflict, defined by rules, that
    results in a quantifiable outcome. Every clause is doing work. <em>Artificial</em> —
    the obstacle is accepted voluntarily; a chess player is not checked by hunger but by a
    rule that says bishops move diagonally, and everyone agrees to pretend that matters.
    <em>Conflict</em> — something opposes the player: an opponent, a clock, a level's
    traps, or the player's own limits. <em>Rules</em> — the boundary that makes the
    contest fair and legible; without rules there is no skill to develop, only effort.
    <em>Quantifiable outcome</em> — you win, lose or score, which lets the next attempt
    mean something.
  </p>
  <p>
    The neighbouring categories sharpen the boundary. A <em>toy</em> has no goal: a ball,
    a sandbox, a physics playground — play without a defined outcome. A <em>puzzle</em>
    has a goal but is static: solved once, its challenge is spent. A <em>film</em> has
    conflict and outcome but no meaningful choices: it plays the same whether you watch
    closely or not. The sweet spot that defines "game" is a goal pursued through
    <em>decisions</em> under rules. Sid Meier's famous compression — a game is "a series of
    interesting decisions" — fails as a definition of every borderline case and succeeds
    as a design compass: where a design asks no decisions, it has stopped being a game in
    the way that matters.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="drag the divider — the neighbours of 'game'" widget="compare" config={gdNeighboursCompare} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> games are defined by winning. Competition is one
  aesthetic among many; cooperative games, building toys with goals, and narrative
  experiences with failure states are all games without adversarial endings. The deep
  structure is the <em>interesting decision under voluntary rules</em> — and even the
  decision can be delegated to randomness, so long as the player owns the odds (poker is
  a game of decisions about chance, not of chance).</p>

  <h2>Mechanics, dynamics, aesthetics: the poker chain</h2>
  <p>
    <em>Mechanics</em> are the rules and systems the designer writes directly: jump
    velocity, health values, resource costs, cooldowns, deck contents, victory conditions.
    They are the only layer under direct authorship, which is why novice designers mistake
    them for the design itself. <em>Dynamics</em> are what the mechanics do when a human
    plays: the emergent chase when one player has a speed advantage, the market that forms
    when resources are tradable, the betrayal a social-deduction rule makes tempting.
    <em>Aesthetics</em> — in MDA's special sense — are what the dynamics feel like:
    tension, mastery, camaraderie, discovery, submission to spectacle.
  </p>
  <p>
    A worked chain shows why the three layers must be held apart. Poker's mechanics are
    thin: ranked hands, betting rounds, hidden information. Its dynamics are rich:
    bluffing, reading betting patterns, pricing risky hands, building a table image. Its
    aesthetics are what people come for — suspense and the razor-edge thrill of a decision
    made with incomplete information. Notice the design insight hiding here: hidden
    information, one mechanic, is doing all the work. Remove it and the dynamics collapse
    into open arithmetic; the aesthetic evaporates. Designers work by finding these
    load-bearing rules. The same analysis explains most "the sequel lost the magic"
    complaints: a mechanic was "modernised" without anyone tracing which dynamics and
    aesthetics it had been quietly powering.
  </p>
  <p>
    The framework's practical edge is direction. Players experience M→D→A; designers must
    work A→D→M. "I want the player to feel desperate" (aesthetic) → "so play must
    sometimes be winnable only by reckless action" (dynamics) → "so resources must
    regenerate slowly, and recovery must cost something irreplaceable" (mechanics). The
    designer who starts at M — "what if the gun shoots three bullets?" — is guessing; the
    one who starts at A is engineering.
  </p>
  <p>
    Johan Huizinga's older idea, the <em>magic circle</em>, explains why the artificiality
    is not a defect. Inside the agreed bubble of rules, moves carry meanings they have
    nowhere else: a moved wooden piece "checks" a king, a yellow card "books" a striker,
    and everyone present treats these as real. Games are among the few places where humans
    collectively agree to let rules, rather than rank or wealth, define what counts —
    which is why cheating feels like a moral injury rather than a mere breach, and why the
    circle's boundary (who agreed to play, and who is being nudged into it) is itself a
    design surface with ethical weight.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="pair the magic circle's moves with their meanings" widget="match" config={gdCircleMatch} />

  <h2>The core loop, decisions and flow</h2>
  <p>
    Good games run on a <em>core loop</em> — the cycle of act, receive feedback, grow, and
    face a harder test that the growth enables. Act, get stronger, fight harder enemy,
    repeat: the loop of nearly every RPG, roguelike and sport. The loop is the game's
    heartbeat, and its tuning is the designer's hardest dial, because the loop only works
    while challenge and skill rise together. Psychologist Mihaly Csikszentmihalyi's
    <em>flow</em> research supplies the model: engagement lives in the narrow channel
    where challenge slightly exceeds skill — enough to demand attention, not enough to
    induce anxiety. Skill outruns challenge and boredom sets in; challenge outruns skill
    and play collapses into frustration.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="pin the flow band on the engagement scale" widget="place" config={gdFlowPlace} />

  <Widget no="2" caption="mastery rises, challenge must follow — flow is the gap" widget="plotter" config={loopCfg} />

  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the green mastery curve has the shape of real
    learning — fast at first, then saturating — while the red challenge line climbs
    steadily. In this sketch the curves cross only once, around hour eight: for most of
    the run mastery leads (the boredom risk), then challenge overtakes (the frustration
    risk). A live game must keep the two interleaved on the player's own schedule, which
    is exactly what difficulty selectors, dynamic difficulty adjustment and well-authored
    progression are for. Underneath every such system sits the atom of the whole
    enterprise — the <em>interesting decision</em>: a choice with visible trade-offs, made
    under uncertainty that is informative rather than arbitrary, with consequences the
    player can read. Rock-paper-scissors has exactly one; chess has a lifetime's worth. A
    ruleset that produces no such decisions — however gorgeous — produces no game.
  </p>
  <p>
    Loops also contain feedback, and its sign matters more than its strength.
    <em>Positive feedback</em> amplifies a lead: the richer get richer (more income buys
    more income), races become foregone conclusions, and sessions end early — thrilling in
    a three-minute match, miserable in a thirty-minute one. <em>Negative feedback</em>
    punishes leading (Mario Kart's rubber-banding gives trailing players better items) and
    keeps outcomes uncertain to the wire, at the cost of quietly devaluing skill.
    Monopoly is the textbook warning: pure positive feedback plus elimination means most
    players' experience is a long, decided-before-the-end looting. Balancing these loops —
    often per game mode, since a tournament and a party night want opposite answers — is
    systems design at its most consequential, and no amount of content polish repairs a
    wrong loop sign.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="file each consequence under its loop" widget="sort-buckets" config={gdLoopsSort} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "a good game is a perfectly fair one — the better
  player should always win." It seems reasonable: unfairness is cheating, and sport
  celebrates the best. But fairness of the <em>rules</em> is different from neutrality of
  the <em>outcome</em>, and many beloved designs deliberately bend the latter. Mario Kart
  hands its best items to the losers; tabletop games add catch-up mechanics; fighting
  games build comeback meters. The designer's question is never "is the outcome fair?" but
  "what does each player feel for how long?" — and keeping four friends in a contest to
  the last lap is a legitimate goal that pure fairness would defeat.</p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="balance the four-player racer" widget="branch" config={gdFairBranch} />

  <h2>Design documents and prototyping</h2>
  <p>
    The industry's medium of thought is the <em>game design document</em> — and its honest
    history is a retreat from bulk. Doorstop GDDs of the 1990s pretended to specify the
    game completely; modern practice prefers living documents: a short concept statement
    (the "elevator pitch" and core fantasy), a description of the core loop, and just
    enough specified systems to build the next test. The document's job is not
    completeness but <em>shared imagination</em> — a team arguing about the same game. The
    single most useful sentence on any page is the core loop's, stated in one breath.
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="fill the design document's real job" widget="cloze" config={gdDocCloze} />
  <p>
    Prototyping is where documents meet truth, and the field's hard-won rule is to test
    the loop as cheaply as possible. Cards, counters and a hallway before code; grey boxes
    before art; an empty room with two cubes before a level. Two findings repeat in every
    studio's history. Mechanics that feel flat on paper almost never bloom in 3D. And
    players will use your mechanics in ways you did not plan — the emergent behaviour that
    is either your game's soul or its exploit economy — which is precisely why the
    argument ends with a playtest and not with the designer.
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="line up the prototyping ladder, cheap to dear" widget="order" config={gdPrototypeOrder} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "mechanics can only really be judged once the game is
  built — prototyping before code is wasted time." It seems reasonable: the finished game
  is the real thing. But mechanics — unlike graphics — are just rules, and rules can be
  run by hand: a spreadsheet economy can be playtested over a lunch table, and a combat
  system's pacing can be felt with tokens. Building first means paying full price
  (art, code, engine work) to learn what a deck of cards would have taught in an
  afternoon — and teaching it too late to act on.</p>
  <Widget no="10" caption="find the faulty line — the studio note" widget="spot-error" config={gdStudioSpot} />

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Give the Salen–Zimmerman definition of a game, and say which clause rules out a toy, which rules out a puzzle, and which rules out a film.</li>
    <li>Name one of poker's mechanics and the aesthetic it powers. What happens if hidden information is removed?</li>
    <li>A designer says: "I want the player to feel like a cornered underdog who can still win." Translate that into a dynamics claim and a mechanics claim.</li>
    <li>In the flow diagram, what two quantities must rise together, and name two interventions a designer has when they separate.</li>
    <li>Monopoly and Mario Kart use opposite feedback signs. Which uses which, and what does each do to a session?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) A system in which players engage in artificial
    conflict, defined by rules, that results in a quantifiable outcome. Artificial (voluntary,
    rule-made obstacles) rules out the aimless ball — the toy. Quantifiable outcome with
    adaptive opposition rules out the solved-once puzzle. Meaningful choices rule out the
    film, which plays identically whether you engage or not. (2) Hidden information powers
    the aesthetics of suspense and bluff-reading. Remove it and the dynamics collapse into
    open arithmetic — everyone knows the hands, so there is nothing to read and nothing to
    misrepresent. (3) Dynamics: the player must frequently be behind on resources or
    position, yet retain a costly path back into contention. Mechanics: slow resource
    regeneration, comeback-friendly catch-up rules, and high-risk high-reward options
    whose downside is real. (4) Challenge and skill (mastery). When they separate, the
    designer can adjust the challenge (difficulty selectors, dynamic difficulty,
    rubber-banding) or the skill curve (better teaching of systems, paced unlocks) — in
    both cases on the player's own schedule. (5) Monopoly is positive feedback (rent buys
    property buys rent): leads compound, most players experience a long decided looting.
    Mario Kart is negative feedback (rubber-banding items for those behind): outcomes
    stay live to the wire at some cost to the value of skill. Neither is "wrong" — they
    serve different aesthetics, which is the whole lesson of loop signs.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Game design engineers emotion through rules, at a distance: the designer never touches the player, only the mechanics.</li>
    <li>A game is a system of artificial conflict under rules with a quantifiable outcome — a toy lacks the goal, a puzzle the adaptivity, a film the decisions.</li>
    <li>MDA: mechanics (written) produce dynamics (played) produce aesthetics (felt); players run M→D→A, designers must argue A→D→M.</li>
    <li>Some rules are load-bearing: one mechanic (like hidden information) can power an entire aesthetic; lose it and the magic leaves with it.</li>
    <li>The core loop needs challenge and skill to rise together — flow lives where they interleave, and feedback-loop signs decide what a session feels like.</li>
    <li>Modern design documents buy shared imagination, not completeness; the core loop should fit in one breath.</li>
    <li>Prototype the loop with paper before code, and let the playtest — not the designer — close the argument.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The next lesson, <em>Engines and the Game Loop</em>, moves from the designer's core
    loop to the engine's loop — the update-and-render cycle that makes all these rules run
    at sixty frames a second. Later, <em>Level and Narrative Design</em> turns flow theory
    into spaces and stories, and <em>Game Production and Business</em> returns to the
    ethics you glimpsed in the magic circle: what monetisation asks of the player's
    consent. Everything downstream assumes the argument you just learned: feeling first,
    behaviour second, rules last — and the playtest gets the final word.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The field's deeper theory lives at the seams of MDA. Player types differ — Richard
    Bartle's classic taxonomy of MUD players (achievers, explorers, socializers, killers)
    remains the standard vocabulary, and a design tuned for one profile bores the others.
    Motivation science reframes "fun": self-determination theory's three needs — autonomy,
    competence, relatedness — map uncomfortably well onto what well-loved games supply,
    and uncomfortably well onto what manipulative ones extract. Uncertainty itself has a
    taxonomy (Costikyan): randomness, hidden information, opponent unpredictability, player
    skill limits — each a different dial with a different feel. And the
    ludology-versus-narratology debate — are games systems or stories? — matured into the
    working position that rules generate narratives, with Ian Bogost's <em>procedural
    rhetoric</em> naming how games argue: a rule system makes claims about how its world
    works, which is why games persuade and why poorly chosen rules quietly say ugly
    things.
  </p>
  <p>
    One boundary is worth drawing because the vocabulary overlaps so confusingly:
    <strong>game design is not game theory</strong>. Game theory is the mathematics of
    strategic conflict among rational, distrustful agents — as one of its popularisers put
    it, it "is not about playing games"; it models players, strategies and payoffs to
    predict what perfectly rational opponents would do. Game design crafts games for
    feeling, for players who are gloriously irrational — tired, hopeful, showing off, on
    tilt. The two fields borrow each other's furniture (poker lives in both), and a
    designer balancing payoffs is doing informal game theory; but flow theory, aesthetics
    and the playtest belong to a discipline the mathematics never aimed to describe. When
    an economist solves a game and a designer breaks one, they are not doing the same job.
  </p>
  <p>
    The open questions are live and ethical as much as technical. Engagement engineering —
    variable-ratio rewards, streaks, social obligation loops — works, and its deliberate
    use in monetisation ("dark patterns") is the discipline's standing scandal; whole
    jurisdictions now debate it. Live-service games make design a running systems problem,
    with economies that players crash in a weekend. And machine-learning opponents raise
    the oldest question in a new form: an opponent that adapts perfectly may be unbeatable
    and unenjoyable — flow theory says the goal was never maximum challenge, and the
    designer's job, as ever, is to build the machine that makes a human feel something on
    purpose.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Hunicke, LeBlanc &amp; Zubek, "MDA: A Formal Approach to Game Design" (2004); IGDA Curriculum Framework; Schell, <em>The Art of Game Design</em>. Source text: OpenStax (https://openstax.org), CC BY 4.0 — adapted for Interactive Atlas.</p>

</TopicPage>
